    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Yesterday Was Wednesday.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple thank-you notes or greetings in cards using one-sentence picture cards. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple thank-you notes or greetings using one-sentence picture cards (e.g., "Thank you!" or "Hello, happy Sunday!"). Can create one-word captions of items for a class book (e.g., "pencil" or "book"). Can write brief captions to describe drawings of days of the week with activities (e.g., "Monday. I need my book and pencil.").
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and displays a large, simple calendar page for "Yesterday was Wednesday." The teacher asks, "What day was yesterday?" (pointing to Wednesday on the calendar) and leads the class to chorally respond, "Yesterday was Wednesday!" Then, the teacher asks, "What day is today?" (pointing to Thursday) and students respond, "Today is Thursday!" The teacher then shows flashcards of simple school activities (e.g., a book for English, a pencil for math) and quickly reviews the associated words, encouraging students to say them as a warm-up for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with "Wednesday" written on it and says, "Wednesday. /w/ Wednesday." emphasizing the /w/ sound. Then, the teacher shows another flashcard with "Friday" and says, "Friday. /f/ Friday." emphasizing the /f/ sound. The teacher then draws a simple picture of a pencil on the board and writes the word "pencil" below it, saying, "This is a pencil. P-E-N-C-I-L. Pencil." The teacher models tracing the word with a finger, showing how to form the letters. Then, the teacher shows a pre-made thank-you card with "Thank you!" and a simple drawing, saying, "Look! Thank you! I can write 'Thank you!'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a book and the word "book"): "Is this a pen or a book?" (Expected answer: "Book!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a flashcard of "Wednesday"): "What sound is at the beginning of 'Wednesday'?" (Expected answer: "/w/!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated daily schedule chart with simple one-sentence captions, pre-printed thank-you notes with spaces for student names/drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays a large chart titled "Our School Week" with columns for each day of the week (Monday-Friday) and simple pictures representing a key activity for each day (e.g., Monday: math book, Tuesday: science beaker, Wednesday: English alphabet, Thursday: drawing, Friday: playground). Below each picture, there is a simple, clear caption in large print, for example: "Monday. We learn math." "Wednesday. We learn English." The teacher points to each day and its activity, reading the caption aloud and encouraging students to repeat. The teacher then focuses on "Wednesday" and says, "Yesterday was Wednesday. We learned English."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to talk about schedules by communicating about daily classroom activities and schedules using appropriate language and turn-taking (e.g., "Today is Monday.") through guided writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What is on Wednesday?" and points to the picture and text. (Expected answer: "English!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a picture of a book. "What is this? Is it science or English?" (Expected answer: "English!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing days of the week, simple sentences using "Yesterday was..." and "Today is...", and one-word captions for school items. They will also practice the grammar: Past simple with the verb "be" ("Yesterday was...") and Present simple ("Today is..."). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, school, math, English, pencil, book. Pronunciation focus: /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" while writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Trace and Match"</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with dotted outlines of the words "Monday," "Wednesday," and "Friday." Next to each word, there are three small pictures: a school building, a math book, and a science beaker. Students first trace the words. Then, the teacher asks them to draw a line from the traced word "Monday" to the picture of the school building, from "Wednesday" to the math book (pretending it's an activity from yesterday), and from "Friday" to the picture that represents something they like (e.g., drawing). The teacher models the first one: "Monday, school."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Sentence Builder with Pictures"</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentence frames and picture prompts. Example: a picture of a school building, then the sentence "Today is _______. I like _______." and a picture of a math book, then "Yesterday was _______. We learn _______." Students choose words from a small word bank (e.g., Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, school, math, English) to complete the sentences based on the pictures. They are encouraged to say the words as they write them, focusing on /w/ and /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Caption Detective"</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 flashcards with pictures of school items (e.g., "pencil," "book," "friend," "school") and a blank strip of paper for each picture. One student draws a simple picture (e.g., a pencil) and writes a one-word caption for it. The partner checks the writing against a word card provided by the teacher, and then tries to guess the word. They take turns drawing and writing. The teacher circulates, offering help with spelling and letter formation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative writing and drawing task: "My Favorite Day Card" and a "Thank You Note."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive two small blank cards. On the first card, they are instructed to draw their favorite school day activity (e.g., drawing on Friday, playing with friends on Thursday) and then write a simple caption. The teacher provides sentence starters and a word bank on the board: "My favorite day is ______." "I like _______." "Yesterday was ______." (e.g., "My favorite day is Friday. I like to draw.") The teacher reminds them of "Yesterday was Wednesday." and encourages them to use days of the week and simple activities. On the second card, students write a simple "Thank you!" greeting to a classmate or the teacher. They can add a small drawing like a heart or a happy face. The teacher ensures students understand to write one clear sentence or phrase per card, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced. Students can share their cards by showing them to a classmate and saying the written words aloud (e.g., "This is my favorite day! Friday!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing simple words and single-sentence greetings, collaborative interaction during sharing, and communicative task completion by creating legible cards.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of student "My Favorite Day Card" and "Thank You Note."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their two cards (My Favorite Day Card and Thank You Note) to the teacher. The teacher quickly reviews the cards for completion and basic legibility. During the production stage, the teacher observes students' ability to write one-word captions and simple sentences with appropriate letter formation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written words/phrases (legibility of letters).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (days of the week, school, pencil, book, friend) in captions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "Yesterday was..." or "Today is..." in simple sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completion of both cards with a simple drawing and written element.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two quick questions by raising their hands or drawing a happy/sad face on a small paper slip:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write 'Thank you!'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write my favorite day?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when writing words?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The writing tasks for this grade level require significant scaffolding and visual support. The "Trace and Match" and "Sentence Builder with Pictures" activities seemed effective in building confidence. Some students still struggle with letter formation and word spacing, which will require continued focus. The "Thank You!" cards were well-received and provided a simple, tangible output. For the next lesson, I need to ensure there are more opportunities for students to *consolidate* their writing skills by using these simple structures in a slightly different context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on this writing practice by having students summarize information about their week and create a small class book or poster featuring their favorite days and activities, integrating their drawings and captions from today's lesson, fostering 21st Century Skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>